From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU: let translated blocks leave out FMAC flag results nobody reads

What it is for:
  Most FMAC results in the game's VU1 transform loops have their MAC and
  status flags replaced by the next FMAC result before any flag
  instruction reads them. Their flag entry still had to be built, queued
  and applied. The game's translated VU code (tools/vu1_translate in
  Protector of Earth Recomp) knows where the flag reads are.

The changes:
  - VU1Interpreter::m_flagElide: set by a translated block around an upper
    instruction the translator marked. fmacFlagsFast then queues nothing
    when every lane takes its fast path (the result adds no U or O flag,
    only Z and S) and Z and S are already in m_flagStickyIssued.
  - m_flagStickyIssued: the sticky Z/S/U/O bits set once every queued
    entry is applied (the status register's, OR each queued status write,
    from the last FSSET on). Kept by appendFmacFlagEntry and queueFsset,
    recomputed from the registers and the ring when run() starts.
  - VU1NativeAccess: blockPairUpper/blockPairUpperLower take a kElide
    template argument (default false); blockCanEnterWithin<kMaxCycles>
    lets such a block start only when its body cannot run out of the
    run's cycle budget, so it never stops between a left-out result and
    the one that replaces it (bounds: kMaxPipelineLatency per pair,
    kMaxXgkickWait for an XGKICK); upperQueuesFmacFlags tells the
    translator which upper words always queue an FMAC entry.
  Code that does not set kElide runs as before.

How it was checked:
  tools/vu1_replay --sum: the same checksums for the 40 VU1 and 60 VU0
  workloads, interpreted and translated (with and without blocks). A
  throwaway build logged every flag read (cycle and register read: 2,806
  MAC and 5,068 clip reads in the VU1 workloads): the same list
  interpreted and translated. ps2xTest 454/454. Instructions in VU1::run
  on the replay, without the resolver: 328.3 M -> 303.3 M; FMAC entries
  queued: 856,920 -> 280,440 (findings/20 s.28).

